.TH WIZ3D-LAUNCH 1 "October 2026" "wiz3d 0.3.1" "Sparky Stereo OS"
.SH NAME
wiz3d-launch \- start a Direct3D 9 Wine game with wiz3D stereo installed in its prefix
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiz3d-launch
[\fB\-\-enable\-3d\fR | \fB\-\-disable\-3d\fR | \fB\-\-native\-stereo\fR \fIlayout\fR [\fB\-\-right\-eye\-first\fR]] [\fB\-\-no\-fallback\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For a Wine game started with WINEPREFIX set.
Puts wiz3D in the prefix's system folders in front of DXVK (DXVK's d3d9.dll is kept as d3d9_real.dll, the rest are
links into /usr/lib/wiz3d), runs \fIcommand\fR through
.BR wiz3d-run (1),
which declares the game's window to KWin as full side by side, left eye first, and takes wiz3D out again when the game ends.
The prefix and the game's folder come out as they went in.
wiz3D's logs and statistics go to WIZ3D_LOG_DIR, by default ~/.local/state/wiz3d.
Paths handed to the Windows side are named the way Wine names them (a drive of the prefix, or \\\\?\\unix\\...),
so a prefix without the Z: drive works too.
A 32-bit prefix (WINEARCH=win32) gets the x86 tree in its system32; a 64-bit prefix gets x64 in system32 and x86 in syswow64.
.PP
A prefix that does not exist yet is made first, the way a game manager makes one: wineboot for the architecture WINEARCH
names (win64 when unset), the system's DXVK through
.BR dxvk-setup (1),
the user folders (Documents, Desktop and the others) as folders inside the prefix instead of links to the real home,
so a game's settings never land in or come from ~/Documents, and no menu entries or file associations from the games it runs
(winemenubuilder.exe is turned off in the prefix).
Z: stays.
Wine Mono and Gecko are not asked for at creation; a game that needs .NET or a browser control installs them later.
So one command installs a game and the same command plays it:
.BR "WINEPREFIX=~/games/x wiz3d-launch --disable-3d -- wine setup.exe" ,
then
.BR "WINEPREFIX=~/games/x wiz3d-launch -- wine game.exe" .
.PP
It needs DXVK in the prefix for Direct3D 9 and says so when it is not there.
.PP
A game with a 3D mode of its own draws both eyes itself, packed into its picture the way 3D televisions take it.
With \fB\-\-native\-stereo\fR \fIlayout\fR wiz3D stays out of the prefix, the system's DXVK is told the packing
(dxvk.stereoInput, through DXVK_CONFIG) and unpacks it to full side by side, left eye first, each eye at full size, and
.BR wiz3d-run (1)
declares the game's window.
The layouts are the frame packings of H.264 Table D-8 and Matroska StereoMode, and the anaglyphs, named as stereo-layer names them:
sideBySide, sideBySideQuincunx, topAndBottom, rows, columns, checkerboard, frameSequential, anaglyphRedCyan, anaglyphGreenMagenta, anaglyphAmberBlue
(the case and dashes do not matter).
Set the game's own 3D option to the layout named here, and its output format to nothing else: the desktop draws what the display needs.
.PP
Wine runs a 32-bit game in a 64-bit prefix in its WoW64 mode, which does not run a 16-bit program at all and leaves some older games crashing.
When the command names the game by path and the game does not run in the 64-bit prefix, on evidence (a 16-bit program by its header,
Wine's own words about the mode in the game's output, or an end within 30 seconds with a status that is not 0),
the game runs again in a 32-bit prefix beside the first (\fIprefix\fR\-win32), made then as above, and the choice is remembered for that
game in ~/.local/state/wiz3d/prefixes.json, where it can be removed.
A quick end with status 0 and nothing said is the game's own doing and is left alone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-3d
Use wiz3D (default).
.TP
.B \-\-disable\-3d
Take wiz3D out of the prefix, if an earlier run left it, and run \fIcommand\fR as it is.
.TP
.BI \-\-native\-stereo " layout"
The game draws both eyes itself in this packing: wiz3D stays out, DXVK unpacks the picture, the window is declared.
.TP
.B \-\-right\-eye\-first
With \fB\-\-native\-stereo\fR: the first view of the packing (left half, top half, even rows, columns, pixels or frames, the first filter) is the right eye.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fallback
Never move a game that fails in the 64-bit prefix to a 32-bit one; WIZ3D_FALLBACK=0 does the same.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
WINEPREFIX
The prefix to use (required); made if it does not exist.
.TP
WINEARCH
win32 or win64 for a prefix made here (default win64).
.TP
WIZ3D_ENABLE_3D
0 does what \fB\-\-disable\-3d\fR does.
.TP
WIZ3D_LOG_DIR
The folder for wiz3D's logs and Statistic.xml.
.TP
WIZ3D_FALLBACK
0 never moves a game to a 32-bit prefix.
.TP
DXVK_CONFIG
Kept, with the stereo input added to it for \fB\-\-native\-stereo\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The game's exit status.
.SH AUTHOR
Sparky Stereo OS, an edition of SparkyLinux, created by Pawe\(sl "pavroo" Pijanowski and Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack).
